{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_submitting-patches","statement":"For trivial issues like changing a word in the documentation, the preferred way to submit the patch is via a GitHub pull request without a Trac ticket.","source_span":"the preferred way to provide the patch is using GitHub pull requests without a Trac ticket"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_submitting-patches","statement":"Django's policy is for contributors to \"claim\" tickets to let other developers know a bug or feature is being worked on.","source_span":"our policy is for contributors to \"claim\" tickets in order to let other developers know that a particular bug or feature is being worked on"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_submitting-patches","statement":"If a ticket's \"Owned by\" field is assigned to \"nobody,\" the ticket is available to be claimed.","source_span":"If it's assigned to \"nobody,\" then it's available to be claimed"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_submitting-patches","statement":"If a ticket has been assigned for weeks or months without activity, it's probably safe to reassign it to yourself.","source_span":"If a ticket has been assigned for weeks or months without any activity, it's probably safe to reassign it to yourself"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_submitting-patches","statement":"For non-trivial changes, contributors have the option to sign and submit a Contributor License Agreement (CLA).","source_span":"you have the option to sign and submit a `Contributor License Agreement`_ clarifying the status of your contribution"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_submitting-patches","statement":"If there's no progress on a claimed ticket for a week or two, another developer may ask the claimer to relinquish the claim.","source_span":"If there's no sign of progress on a particular claimed ticket for a week or two, another developer may ask you to relinquish the ticket claim"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_submitting-patches","statement":"For small changes such as documentation typos or quick bug fixes, contributors don't need to claim tickets and can submit changes directly.","source_span":"In the case of small changes, such as typos in the documentation or small bugs that will only take a few minutes to fix, you don't need to jump through the hoops of claiming tickets. Submit your changes directly"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_submitting-patches","statement":"A good fix should include a regression test to validate the fixed behavior and prevent the problem from recurring.","source_span":"A good fix should also include a :doc:`regression test... to validate the behavior that has been fixed and to prevent the problem from arising again"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_submitting-patches","statement":"If code adds a new feature or modifies the behavior of an existing feature, the change should also contain documentation.","source_span":"If the code adds a new feature, or modifies the behavior of an existing feature, the change should also contain documentation"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_submitting-patches","statement":"Patches submitted via Trac (not pull request) should use the format returned by the `git diff` command.","source_span":"Submit patches in the format returned by the ``git diff`` command"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_submitting-patches","statement":"Patch files should be named with a .diff extension so the ticket tracker applies correct syntax highlighting.","source_span":"Name the patch file with a ``.diff`` extension; this will let the ticket tracker apply correct syntax highlighting"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_submitting-patches","statement":"Patches should not be put in the ticket description or comment unless it's a single line patch.","source_span":"Please *don't* put the patch in the ticket description or comment unless it's a single line patch"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_submitting-patches","statement":"AI-generated submissions that show no evidence of manual verification may be closed without review, and repeated low-quality contributions may lead to restricted participation in Django's development process.","source_span":"Submissions that show no evidence of manual verification may be closed without review, and repeated low-quality contributions may lead to restricted participation in Django's development process"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_submitting-patches","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What's the preferred way to submit a fix for a trivial typo in Django's documentation?","answer":"Use a GitHub pull request without opening a Trac ticket."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_submitting-patches","fact_refs":[2],"question":"How can you tell if a Django Trac ticket is available to be claimed?","answer":"Check the ticket's \"Owned by\" section — if it's assigned to \"nobody,\" it's available to claim."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_submitting-patches","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What should you do if a Django ticket has been assigned to someone for weeks or months with no activity?","answer":"It's probably safe to reassign the ticket to yourself."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_submitting-patches","fact_refs":[10,11],"question":"What file extension and submission method should be used for a Django patch attached to a Trac ticket?","answer":"The patch should be in `git diff` format, named with a .diff extension, and attached via the ticket's \"attach file\" button rather than pasted into the description or comment (unless it's a single line)."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_submitting-patches","fact_refs":[12],"question":"What happens to AI-generated Django contributions that show no evidence of manual verification?","answer":"They may be closed without review, and repeated low-quality contributions can lead to restricted participation in Django's development process."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_submitting-patches","fact_refs":[6],"question":"Do you need to claim a ticket before fixing a small typo in Django's docs?","answer":"No — for small changes like typos or quick bug fixes, you can submit your changes directly without claiming a ticket first."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_unit-tests","statement":"Django's own test suite is located in the `tests` directory of the code base.","source_span":"Django comes with a test suite of its own, in the ``tests`` directory of the code base"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_unit-tests","statement":"Django's policy is to make sure all tests pass at all times.","source_span":"It's our policy to make sure all tests pass at all times"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_unit-tests","statement":"The quickstart for running Django's tests starts with forking Django on GitHub, then cloning the fork into a directory named django-repo and running ./runtests.py from the tests subdirectory.","source_span":"$ git clone https://github.com/YourGitHubName/django.git django-repo\n   $ cd django-repo/tests\n   $ python -m pip install -e ..\n   $ python -m pip install -r requirements/py3.txt\n   $ ./runtests.py"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_unit-tests","statement":"If installing an optional test dependency like pylibmc fails, you can comment out its line in requirements/py3.txt and tests requiring pylibmc will be skipped automatically.","source_span":"if installing ``pylibmc`` fails, comment out its line in ``requirements/py3.txt`` and continue with ``./runtests.py``. Tests that require ``pylibmc`` will be skipped automatically"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_unit-tests","statement":"Django provides a sample settings module that uses the SQLite database to help users get started running tests.","source_span":"Django provides and uses a sample settings module that uses the SQLite database"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_unit-tests","statement":"By default, tox runs the test suite with SQLite plus black, blacken-docs, flake8, isort, lint-docs, zizmor, and the documentation spelling checker.","source_span":"By default, ``tox`` runs the test suite with the bundled test settings file for SQLite, ``black``, ``blacken-docs``, ``flake8``, ``isort``, ``lint-docs``, ``zizmor``, and the documentation spelling checker"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_unit-tests","statement":"To run Django's tests on Python 3.12 with PostgreSQL via tox, the command is `tox -e py312-postgres -- --settings=my_postgres_settings`.","source_span":"$ tox -e py312-postgres -- --settings=my_postgres_settings"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_unit-tests","statement":"JavaScript tests aren't run by default via tox because they require Node.js to be installed and aren't necessary for the majority of patches.","source_span":"The JavaScript tests aren't run by default using ``tox`` because they require ``Node.js`` to be installed and aren't necessary for the majority of patches"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_unit-tests","statement":"To run the JavaScript tests using tox, run `tox -e javascript`, which runs `npm install` and then `npm test`.","source_span":"$ tox -e javascript\n\nThis command runs ``npm install`` to ensure test requirements are up to date and then runs ``npm test``"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_unit-tests","statement":"django-docker-box allows running Django's test suite across all supported databases and Python versions.","source_span":"`django-docker-box`_ allows you to run the Django's test suite across all supported databases and python versions"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_unit-tests","statement":"The included settings module for running Django's tests with SQLite is tests/test_sqlite.py.","source_span":"The included settings module (``tests/test_sqlite.py``) allows you to run the test suite using SQLite"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_unit-tests","statement":"A custom test settings module must define a \"default\" database and a database with the alias \"other,\" which must use the same backend as default but have a different name.","source_span":"A database with the alias ``other``. The ``other`` database is used to test that queries can be directed to different databases. This database should use the same backend as the ``default``, and it must have a different name"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_unit-tests","fact_refs":[0],"question":"Where is Django's own test suite located in the code base?","answer":"In the `tests` directory of the code base."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_unit-tests","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What are the basic quickstart steps to run Django's tests after forking the repo?","answer":"Clone your fork into a directory named django-repo, cd into django-repo/tests, install Django and the requirements, then run `./runtests.py`."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_unit-tests","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What should you do if installing the optional pylibmc test dependency fails?","answer":"Comment out its line in requirements/py3.txt and continue running ./runtests.py; tests requiring pylibmc will be skipped automatically."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_unit-tests","fact_refs":[5],"question":"Besides the SQLite test suite, what tools does tox run by default in Django?","answer":"black, blacken-docs, flake8, isort, lint-docs, zizmor, and the documentation spelling checker."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_unit-tests","fact_refs":[8],"question":"How do you run Django's JavaScript unit tests using tox?","answer":"Run `tox -e javascript`, which runs `npm install` to update requirements and then runs `npm test`."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_unit-tests","fact_refs":[11],"question":"What two databases must a custom Django test settings module define, and how must they relate to each other?","answer":"A \"default\" database and a database aliased \"other\"; the \"other\" database must use the same backend as default but have a different name."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_working-with-git","statement":"After installing Git, you should configure your name and email with `git config --global user.name \"Your Real Name\"` and `git config --global user.email \"you@email.com\"`.","source_span":"$ git config --global user.name \"Your Real Name\"\n  $ git config --global user.email \"you@email.com\""}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_working-with-git","statement":"The `user.name` git config value should be the contributor's real name, not their GitHub nick.","source_span":"Note that ``user.name`` should be your real name, not your GitHub nick"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_working-with-git","statement":"To create a new branch for working on a ticket based on upstream/main, run `git checkout -b ticket_xxxxx upstream/main`.","source_span":"git checkout -b ticket_xxxxx upstream/main"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_working-with-git","statement":"For a fix on the 1.4 branch, the branch is created with `git checkout -b ticket_xxxxx_1_4 upstream/stable/1.4.x`.","source_span":"git checkout -b ticket_xxxxx_1_4 upstream/stable/1.4.x"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_working-with-git","statement":"Work is published to GitHub by running `git push origin ticket_xxxxx`.","source_span":"git push origin ticket_xxxxx"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_working-with-git","statement":"To change the message of the most recent commit, run `git commit --amend`.","source_span":"git commit --amend"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_working-with-git","statement":"To reword one of the last three commits, run `git rebase -i HEAD~3`, which opens an editor to change \"pick\" to \"reword\" on the target commit.","source_span":"git rebase -i HEAD~3"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_working-with-git","statement":"After rewriting a commit that has already been pushed to GitHub, you need to force-push with `git push --force-with-lease origin ticket_xxxxx`.","source_span":"git push --force-with-lease origin ticket_xxxxx"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_working-with-git","statement":"A good pull request has well-formed commit messages: a summary line followed by paragraphs wrapped at 72 characters.","source_span":"well-formed messages for each commit: a summary line and then paragraphs wrapped at 72 characters thereafter"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_working-with-git","statement":"Tests are always needed for a pull request, except for documentation-only changes.","source_span":"documentation and tests, if needed -- actually tests are always needed, except for documentation changes"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_working-with-git","statement":"The main and stable/A.B.x branches in the django/django repository are examples of \"public branches\" whose history should never change.","source_span":"Good examples of public branches are the ``main`` and ``stable/A.B.x`` branches in the ``django/django`` repository"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_working-with-git","statement":"A \"topic branch\" (such as ticket_xxxxx) can have its history freely rewritten with git rebase, since others shouldn't base their work on it.","source_span":"the above branch is called a \"topic branch\" in Git parlance. You are free to rewrite the history of this branch, by using ``git rebase``"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_working-with-git","fact_refs":[0,1],"question":"What two git config commands should you run after installing Git, and what should the user.name value be?","answer":"Run `git config --global user.name \"Your Real Name\"` and `git config --global user.email \"you@email.com\"`; user.name should be your real name, not your GitHub nick."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_working-with-git","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What command creates a new branch for working on a Django ticket based on upstream/main?","answer":"`git checkout -b ticket_xxxxx upstream/main`."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_working-with-git","fact_refs":[4],"question":"How do you publish your work on a topic branch to GitHub?","answer":"Run `git push origin ticket_xxxxx`."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_working-with-git","fact_refs":[6],"question":"How do you reword one of the last three commits in a branch?","answer":"Run `git rebase -i HEAD~3`, then change \"pick\" to \"reword\" on the target commit's line in the editor that opens."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_working-with-git","fact_refs":[9],"question":"When are tests required for a Django pull request?","answer":"Tests are always needed, except for documentation-only changes."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_working-with-git","fact_refs":[10],"question":"Which branches in the django/django repository are examples of \"public branches\" that should never have their history changed?","answer":"The `main` and `stable/A.B.x` branches."}
